id: FindEntitiesByTextContent
name: ᝯㄝₓ Find & Replace Entities by Text Content
metadata:
  description: "Which entities in your library mention this text? Stash cannot be asked - it will filter a scene list on a scene's own details and a performer list on a performer's own details, and nothing at all on whichever custom fields an entity happens to carry. This plugin is that one question, with one box to type it into.\n\nRun it from Settings - Tasks - Plugin Tasks. Type what to look for, turn on the entity types you want searched - they all start off, so nothing is read until you say so - and press Search. It looks in every text field of every Scene, Image, Gallery, Performer, Studio, Group and Tag you turned on: titles, details, descriptions, synopses, aliases, URLs, directors, photographers, and the names and values of custom fields.\n\nResults arrive as they are found, one line per entity: the entity with its id, which attributes matched and how many times each, and the text around the first match with the match marked. Click one to open it in a new tab. Counters and a cursor across the top say how far it has read, out of how many, and that it is still going.\n\nA second row of filters fills in as the search finds attributes to offer - Title, Details, Aliases, custom field names and values, and the rest - one for every attribute something has actually matched in. Turning one off hides the entities that only matched there and drops that attribute from the lines that also matched elsewhere, so a line never claims a match it is hiding. The two rows act at different moments: entity types decide what is read, attributes decide what is shown.\n\nSearch becomes Pause while it runs and Resume after, so a long search can be stopped and picked up rather than started again. When the list on screen is full it pauses itself and the button reads Continue, which clears the screen and carries on - Copy log keeps every result either way, including the ones the screen no longer shows. Refresh throws the results away and searches again.\n\nThe dialog can remember the entity types you chose, and can keep the last few things you searched for and offer them back in the box itself as you type. Case-sensitive is remembered on its own, without being asked for. Both live in this browser rather than on the server, both are off until you ask for them, and setting the number of remembered searches to zero is also how that list is cleared.\n\nTick Replace and a second line appears: what to put in place of the text you searched for. The Replace button then rewrites every entity on the list, in the attributes still shown - so the list you are looking at is the plan, and narrowing it with the attribute filters narrows what a press covers. Matching follows Case-sensitive, as the search did, and the replacement goes in exactly as typed; an empty box deletes the text it finds. Every entity it rewrites gets its own [EDIT] line in the log, which is the only kind of line that says your library changed. While it runs, Stop is the only way out of the dialog - it ends after the batch in flight, what landed stays landed, and closing is not offered because the writes would carry on unseen. Undo puts back what that press changed, field by field, while the dialog stays open. The box remembers what you have replaced with, in its own list, using the same number-kept setting as the search box.\n\nWith Replace off nothing is written to your library at any point: it is a read and a list of links. BACK UP YOUR DATABASE BEFORE THE FIRST REPLACE - it rewrites text across your library.\n\nMatching is a plain substring, so a short word matches inside longer ones - the text around every result is there so you can see which it was. It ignores case unless you tick Case-sensitive, which applies to a replacement as well, takes effect on the next search, and is remembered in this browser. Which fields your Stash actually has is settled by asking it rather than assumed, so a field this plugin looks for and your server does not have is skipped instead of failing the whole search.\n\nScene markers are not searched: a marker carries a title and no page of its own to open.\n\nRequires Stash 0.31.0 or newer."
